WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.375
ALICIA WILLIAMS: One of the
benefits of a data warehouse,

00:00:02.375 --> 00:00:04.710
like BigQuery, is the
improved simplicity and speed

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:08.160
of bringing data to your
analysts and decision-makers.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.140
Data needs vary
across a company based

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.210
on organizational
function, geography,

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:13.830
and more, so it's
important to be

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:16.379
able to provide customized
access to your users

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:19.170
without adding unnecessary risk.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:21.300
In today's episode of
"BigQuery Spotlight,"

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.790
we're showing you how to
create authorized views, which

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:26.700
give you the ability to define
and share a specific subset

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.710
or view of your BigQuery data.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:29.490
Stay tuned.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:33.394
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:36.322 --> 00:00:40.270
BigQuery organizes data into
containers called datasets.

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:42.670
These datasets function
like top-level folders

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:46.650
that organize and control
access to the underlying tables.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.540
In our episode, "Managing
Access with Cloud IAM,"

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:51.100
we talked about how
to manage access

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.080
at the data set and
table level using

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.180
the Identity and Access
Management Service,

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:56.740
or Cloud IAM.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:59.190
If you want to check it
out, we've linked it below.

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:02.715
Cloud IAM can restrict access
down to the table level.

00:01:02.715 --> 00:01:04.840
But what if you want to
restrict access for members

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.540
to parts of a table?

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.860
In BigQuery, one way
to accomplish this task

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:11.410
is by creating authorized views.

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:13.180
An authorized view
allows you to share

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:16.480
the results of a query with
a specific user or group

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:19.120
without giving that user
access to the tables that

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.380
were queried.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.500
For example, let's say
as a BigQuery data admin,

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.410
you wanted to give an analyst at
your company access to a table

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:28.870
containing your
service request data,

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:31.750
but you wanted to remove the
full address of the service

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:33.160
location.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:35.620
You could write a query that
didn't include the address

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.410
column and then save the
results as an authorized view

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:40.300
to share with the analyst.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.880
Creating an authorized view
follows a defined series

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:44.240
of steps.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.200
Step one, start with
your source dataset.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.190
This is the dataset
with the sensitive data

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:50.890
you don't want to share.

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:54.460
Step two, create a separate
dataset to store the view.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:56.340
Authorized views
require the source data

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.395
sit in a separate
dataset from the view.

00:01:58.395 --> 00:01:59.770
Don't worry about
the reason why.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:01.890
You'll see in step six.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:05.480
Step three, create a
view in the new dataset.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:07.410
In the new dataset,
you create the view

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:09.870
you intend to share
with your data analysts.

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.000
This view is created
using a SQL query that

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.940
includes only the data
the analysts need to see.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:19.200
Step four, assign access
controls to the project.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.330
In order to query the
view, your analyst

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:23.700
needs permission to run queries.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.980
Assigning your analyst
the BigQuery user role

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:27.870
gives them this ability.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:29.910
This access does not
give them the ability

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:33.890
to view or query any
datasets within the project.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:36.440
Step five, assign
access controls

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.810
to the dataset
containing the view.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:40.790
In order for your analysts
to query the view,

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:42.290
they need to be
granted the BigQuery

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:44.690
data viewer role for that
specific dataset that

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:46.370
contains the view.

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:49.040
And finally, step six,
authorize the view

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.170
to access the source dataset.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:54.352
This gives the view itself
access to the source data.

00:02:54.352 --> 00:02:56.060
We need to do this
because the view takes

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:58.028
on the permissions of
the person using it,

00:02:58.028 --> 00:03:00.320
and since the analyst doesn't
have access to the source

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:02.180
table, they'd
otherwise get an error

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:04.060
if they tried to
query this view.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:06.720
We're going to jump into the
console and see how it's done.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.070
You can follow along in your
own BigQuery sandbox, which

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:11.210
you can set up for free
following the instructions

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:12.920
in the video link below.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:14.870
We're using two
sandboxes in order

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:16.940
to represent the
perspectives of both the data

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.250
admin and the analyst.

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:20.060
OK.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.812
Starting out as the
data admin, step one,

00:03:22.812 --> 00:03:24.770
you'll need a dataset to
store your full source

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:27.500
data containing the
sensitive address field.

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:29.750
From the BigQuery Console,
select your project

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.090
and click CREATE DATASET.

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:35.210
Give your dataset a name
and click the Create dataset

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:36.900
button.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.990
Now you'll populate a table
within the dataset using a SQL

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:41.100
query.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.710
Click COMPOSE NEW QUERY
and copy and paste

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:45.960
the Source Data Query that's
linked in the description

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.240
below into the Query Editor.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:51.030
Before running the query,
go into Query Settings

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:53.340
and check the box for
Set a destination table

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.600
for query results.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:58.570
Verify your project and
dataset name are selected,

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:01.430
then name your table
service_requests.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:03.010
Click Save.

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:04.930
Now click Run.

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:07.330
When the query completes,
you can select your new table

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:09.790
in the left-hand nav
and click Preview to see

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:12.560
that the data was populated.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.470
Step two, time to create a
separate dataset where you'll

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:17.730
put the view for your analyst.

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:20.149
You'll follow the same
steps we just performed.

00:04:20.149 --> 00:04:23.390
Select your project and
click CREATE DATASET.

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:27.590
Name your dataset shared_views
and click Create dataset.

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:31.100
Step three, now you'll create
the view for your analysts.

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:33.740
Click COMPOSE NEW QUERY
and copy and paste

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:35.540
the View Query linked
in the description

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:37.790
below into the Query Editor.

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:40.490
This query selects all the
columns from the source data,

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:43.130
except for the
sensitive address field.

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:44.930
Now click save view.

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:47.570
Make sure your project
and dataset are selected

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:48.680
and give the view a name.

00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:53.510
Click SAVE.

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:57.320
You can see that the view is now
populated in the left-hand nav.

00:04:57.320 --> 00:05:00.500
We have our source table
and our analyst view set up.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:03.470
Time to start assigning access
controls for our analyst.

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:05.720
Step four, give the
analyst permission

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:08.450
to query in the project by
assigning the BigQuery user

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:09.620
role.

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:14.360
From the navigation menu, head
to IAM &amp; Admin and click IAM.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:16.290
Click ADD.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:19.140
In the members field, enter
the analyst's email address,

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:21.150
or as we are using,
a Google Group

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.230
that contains your analysts.

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:25.520
In the roles field,
click Select a role

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:27.330
and choose BigQuery User.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:28.770
Click Save.

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:33.000
Head back to BigQuery by using
the search bar in the console.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:36.060
Step five, next, you'll
give the analyst permission

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:39.090
to read the dataset
that contains the view.

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:43.170
Select the shared_views dataset
and then click SHARE DATASET.

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:44.670
Click Add members.

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:48.030
Again, enter the email or
group for your analysts.

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:50.550
Select the role
BigQuery Data Viewer,

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:54.640
then click Add, and
finally, click Done.

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:57.340
Step six, you need to
give the view itself

00:05:57.340 --> 00:05:59.530
access to the source dataset.

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:02.440
This is what is meant
by an authorized view.

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:05.360
From the source dataset,
click SHARE DATASET.

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:08.720
And then this time, head to
the AUTHORIZED VIEWS section.

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:10.870
Change the dataset
to shared_views

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:13.280
and select your
view for the table.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:16.060
Click Add and then click Done.

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:19.100
Your authorized view
configuration is now complete.

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:20.770
Let's flip over to
the analyst's account

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:23.230
to see the result from
their perspective.

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:26.170
The analyst is logged in and
has selected the same project,

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:28.000
analytics-source-data.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.820
Let's verify what data
they are able to view.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:33.730
As you can see, the analyst is
able to both access the view

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:35.830
and run queries
over it, but when

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:40.210
they try to view the source
table, they do not have access.

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:42.310
Now you're ready to
use this same process

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:45.040
to create any number of
authorized views customized

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:48.130
for your groups and
users at your company.

00:06:48.130 --> 00:06:49.930
If you want to learn
more about BigQuery,

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:51.457
check out the rest
of our playlist.

00:06:51.457 --> 00:06:53.290
And don't forget to
subscribe to our channel

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:55.450
on YouTube for
other great content.

00:06:55.450 --> 00:06:57.700
Look out for our next episode
of "BigQuery Spotlight,"

00:06:57.700 --> 00:06:59.260
and remember, stay curious.

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:02.610
[MUSIC PLAYING]

